7] [17592186401908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401908 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.3x-0.1y=0.6 \\\\ 0.6x+0.8y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d7c59ff-df86-5863-a05a-c20fb84b2ead" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401908 #edb/ref 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.3x-0.1y=0.6 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 0.6x+0.8y=0.2 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 \\\\ 6x+8y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we divide the second equation by $(-2),$ we will get a $-3x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 &  \\\\ 6x+8y=2 & |\\,÷(-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 \\\\ -3x-4y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3x}-y & =6 \\\\ -\\cancel{3x}-4y & =-1 \\\\ (-y-4y) & =(6-1) \\\\ -5y & =5 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-1$ into the equation $3x-y=6$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y & =6 \\\\ 3x-(-1) & =6 \\\\ 3x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $y=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186401914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401909 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e422abe5-7836-5aa6-bfb2-34896476a4d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401910 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=\\frac{4}{3},$ $y=-2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401910 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401910 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c29c57bf-e265-52a1-bca7-e9f5dad2f3f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401911 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=1,$ $y=-3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "211a2e7b-9745-58a0-94ae-5560db869797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401912 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=\\frac{7}{3},$ $y=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "862aa3fb-5e83-5622-8209-8caba2d722df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401913 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=2,$ $y=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30d0a7a2-ddd3-5355-ab33-3b6c7d057ae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401914 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=\\frac{5}{3},$ $y=-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0284c37f-87d7-5181-9665-5eb50a2bc860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-31919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401988 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det(A)\\ne 0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, then $A$ is $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. invertible\nII. **not ** invertible\nIII. singular" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "561c404c-366e-5e58-b3aa-1094d48ff6e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401988 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option I gives a true statement:\n\n- *If $det(A)\\ne 0$ , then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186401991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401989 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3cd4133-bfb2-57ca-9d13-24b0af1c763d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401990 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26bd5bb7-0a8a-5edb-975d-4fd5f046599e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401991 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401991 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401991 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08992256-6bf5-5ba6-a198-d2f970ef2ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401992 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401992 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401992 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52bbf42f-38cf-5d5c-af4e-ee00925fa061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401993 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61cc3875-ca37-5eff-9f67-f6c05f654183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401994 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401994 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401994 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5af54dbb-f852-501b-bb4f-1e25a922f13c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402009 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f65d67ed-b94b-50cc-87d4-9aa7130f7547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402009 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402010 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "94d6271a-24bd-58c0-9553-83b2dc10742e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402011 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17